- RegisterLegRequest {requestId, legId, origin, destination}
- RegisterBagRequest {requestId, bagTag, legIds: List<String>}
- LoadRequest {requestId, expectedVersion, bagTags: List<String>}
- SealRequest {requestId, expectedVersion}
- ArriveRequest {requestId, bagTags: List<String>}

Responses: I'll return Maps from service or define records. Records are cleaner:
- LegResponse {legId, origin, destination, status, version}
- BagResponse {bagTag, currentLocation, nextLegIndex, status, itinerary: List<ItineraryItem>}
- ItineraryItem {seq, legId, origin, destination}
- LoadResponse {legId, version, loaded: List<String>}
- SealResponse {legId, status, version, manifest: List<String>}
- ArriveResponse {legId, status, version, arrived: List<String>}
- TraceResponse {bagTag, status, currentLocation, nextLegIndex, itinerary, loadedLegId}
- ManifestResponse {legId, status, version, manifest}

Validation annotations: @NotBlank, @NotNull, @Size(min=1,max=5), @Size(min=1,max=20).

Write one DTO file with nested records? Java allows multiple records in one file only if not public... I'll make a single public final class `Dtos` with nested records, or separate files. Separate files are cleaner but many. I'll use one file `BaggageDtos.java` with nested public static records. Actually records nested in an interface/class are implicitly static. Let me do a final class with nested records.

